    "body": "Migrates `src/web` to Jane Street Core \u2014 168 files, the largest library in the stack. Two commits: typed equality (104 sites) then the flip.\n\n`src/web/dune` has **four** OCaml stanzas \u2014 libraries `web` and `workerServer` plus executables `main` and `worker` \u2014 and all four need the flag. It also compiles 17 `.ml`/`.mli` example files under `exercises/` and `derivation/`, which are easy to miss when sweeping for `.re`.\n\n**Poly usage is much higher here than in `src/language`** \u2014 42 of 223 modules take `open Poly`, versus 4 of 112. View and model code compares whole variants and records constantly, and Poly is exactly the semantics that code already had. It was applied compiler-driven rather than pre-emptively.\n\n**Two things reviewers should look at closely:**\n- `DerivationExercise`'s key parser *relies on* `String.index` raising, and catches `Not_found`. Core's `index_exn` raises `Not_found_s`, which that handler would silently stop matching \u2014 so it uses `Stdlib.String.index` and `Stdlib.Not_found` on purpose.\n- Core's `Option` has `some` but no `none`, and doesn't re-declare the `None`/`Some` constructors. Where the original wrote `Option.None` *deliberately* to disambiguate from another `None` in scope, it's now `Stdlib.Option.none` to preserve that.\n\n## Verification\n`make dev` clean \u00b7 `make test-quick` green (3448) \u00b7 `dune build --profile release src/web` warning-free.\n\n\ud83e\udd16 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
